cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  The AI Trust Funnel: Spectrum-First Onboarding for Multi-AI
  Consumer Products
message: >-
  If you use this paper or its associated data, please cite
  it as below.
type: article
authors:
  - given-names: Chanmin
    family-names: Kim
    affiliation: Eliary Inc.
    email: chanmin@eliary.com
abstract: >-
  Multi-AI consumer products face an AI-resistance challenge
  at onboarding. We document the Three-Stage AI Trust Funnel
  (Stages 1–3 measurable; Stages 0/4/5 stated explicitly as
  instrumentation gaps) across 3,899 user sessions over the
  14-day public-access window (April 27 – May 10, 2026) on
  Lucid, a four-vendor multi-AI personality-test platform.
  We frame five testable hypotheses for the 4.28%
  completer-to-verify conversion gap. The analyzed population
  is 65.9% AI-skeptical (score_ait ≤ 3, n=1,032 of 1,566),
  disconfirming the implicit selection assumption that
  spectrum-first onboarding draws AI-positive users. The
  central empirical finding is the segment-conversion
  comparison: AI-positive sessions phone-verify at 6.41%
  (n=78), AI-skeptical at 3.78% (n=1,032); dominance ratio
  1.696 places the result in the H1 DOMINANT branch
  (≥ 1.5 prespecified threshold) — residual AI resistance is
  a leading explanatory factor for the conversion gap. The
  paper's contribution is the funnel formalization, the
  dual-function items methodology, and the five-hypothesis
  framework for the conversion gap.
keywords:
  - AI resistance
  - multi-AI chat
  - onboarding
  - funnel decomposition
  - signaling theory
  - personality assessment
  - spectrum-first onboarding
